### Example usage

```tsx
import CheckoutScreen from './screens/Checkout/CheckoutScreen';
import type { CartItem, DeliveryAddress } from './screens/Checkout/types';

const cartItems: CartItem[] = [
  { id: '1', name: 'Wireless Earbuds', price: 29.99, quantity: 1 },
  { id: '2', name: 'Phone Case', price: 12.5, quantity: 2 },
];

function App() {
  async function handlePay(address: DeliveryAddress) {
    console.log('Submitting order to', address);
    // call your backend / Telegram invoice API here
  }

  return (
    <CheckoutScreen
      cartItems={cartItems}
      currency="USD"
      onBack={() => console.log('go back to cart')}
      onSubmit={handlePay}
    />
  );
}

export default App;
```

Notes:
- Uses Telegram theme CSS variables (`--tg-theme-*`) with sensible fallbacks so it blends into whatever theme the user's Telegram client applies, light or dark.
- `onSubmit` is async so you can plug in a real payment call (e.g. Telegram's `openInvoice` or your backend) — rejecting the promise surfaces an inline error instead of crashing.
- Validation is client-side only; wire up server-side checks before charging anything for real.
